window.__labLoaded({"id":"9.2","title":"Assumptions and Normality: Can I Trust This Test?","goal":"Check normality with the Shapiro-Wilk test and a normal Q-Q plot for two samples of 20 values from the lesson, one roughly normal (exam scores) and one skewed (response times).","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the two samples","text":"Exam scores look roughly bell-shaped. Response times have a long tail of a few very slow values. A look at the mean versus the median already hints at the difference.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nexam = np.array([62, 65, 68, 70, 71, 72, 73, 74, 75, 76, 77, 78, 79, 80, 82, 83, 85, 87, 90, 94])\nresp = np.array([1.2, 1.4, 1.5, 1.7, 1.9, 2.0, 2.2, 2.3, 2.6, 2.8, 3.1, 3.5, 3.9, 4.6, 5.4, 6.8, 8.9, 12.5, 18.7, 31.2])\n\nfor name, x in [('exam scores', exam), ('response times', resp)]:\n    print(f'{name:15s} mean = {x.mean():.2f}, median = {np.median(x):.2f}')","out":"exam scores     mean = 77.05, median = 76.50\nresponse times  mean = 5.91, median = 2.95","figs":[]},{"title":"Shapiro-Wilk test of normality","text":"Shapiro-Wilk starts from the assumption that the data are normal. A small p-value (below 0.05) is evidence against normality; a large one only means we found no evidence against it.","code":"for name, x in [('exam scores', exam), ('response times', resp)]:\n    w = stats.shapiro(x)\n    p_text = f'{w.pvalue:.4f}' if w.pvalue >= 0.0001 else '< 0.0001'\n    verdict = 'no evidence against normality' if w.pvalue >= 0.05 else 'evidence of non-normality'\n    print(f'{name:15s} W = {w.statistic:.3f}, p = {p_text}  ->  {verdict}')","out":"exam scores     W = 0.993, p = 0.9998  ->  no evidence against normality\nresponse times  W = 0.641, p = < 0.0001  ->  evidence of non-normality","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the normal Q-Q plots","text":"A Q-Q plot lines up the sorted data against what a normal sample would give. Points close to the straight line mean roughly normal; a curve that bends away, as with the response times, signals skew.","code":"plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.subplot(1, 2, 1)\nstats.probplot(exam, dist='norm', plot=plt)\nplt.title('Exam scores')\nplt.subplot(1, 2, 2)\nstats.probplot(resp, dist='norm', plot=plt)\nplt.title('Response times')\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":["9.2-3.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"exam <- c(62, 65, 68, 70, 71, 72, 73, 74, 75, 76, 77, 78, 79, 80, 82, 83, 85, 87, 90, 94)\nresp <- c(1.2, 1.4, 1.5, 1.7, 1.9, 2.0, 2.2, 2.3, 2.6, 2.8, 3.1, 3.5, 3.9, 4.6, 5.4, 6.8, 8.9, 12.5, 18.7, 31.2)\nshapiro.test(exam)\nshapiro.test(resp)\npar(mfrow = c(1, 2))\nqqnorm(exam, main = \"Exam scores\"); qqline(exam)\nqqnorm(resp, main = \"Response times\"); qqline(resp)","expect":"exam: W = 0.993, p-value = 0.9998 (approx); response times: W = 0.641, p-value = 8.2e-06 (below 0.0001)","note":"shapiro.test prints W and the p-value; the Q-Q plots are drawn by qqnorm plus qqline."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore (Plots: Normality plots with tests)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / exam resp.\nBEGIN DATA\n62 1.2  65 1.4  68 1.5  70 1.7  71 1.9  72 2.0  73 2.2  74 2.3  75 2.6  76 2.8\n77 3.1  78 3.5  79 3.9  80 4.6  82 5.4  83 6.8  85 8.9  87 12.5  90 18.7  94 31.2\nEND DATA.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=exam resp\n  /PLOT=NPPLOT\n  /STATISTICS=NONE.","note":"The Tests of Normality table shows Shapiro-Wilk (statistic and Sig.) and also Kolmogorov-Smirnov; use the Shapiro-Wilk row. NPPLOT adds the normal Q-Q plots."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 20\ngenerate exam = real(word(\"62 65 68 70 71 72 73 74 75 76 77 78 79 80 82 83 85 87 90 94\", _n))\ngenerate resp = real(word(\"1.2 1.4 1.5 1.7 1.9 2.0 2.2 2.3 2.6 2.8 3.1 3.5 3.9 4.6 5.4 6.8 8.9 12.5 18.7 31.2\", _n))\nswilk exam resp\nqnorm exam\nqnorm resp","note":"swilk prints W and V statistics plus a z and Prob>z; the Prob>z column is the p-value (it is a close approximation to the others, and the Shapiro-Wilk W matches). qnorm draws a normal Q-Q plot."},"try":["Replace the largest response time (31.2) with 6.0. Predict whether W moves toward 1 or away, then run it.","Take the logarithm of the response times (np.log) and run Shapiro-Wilk again. Does the skew go away?"]});
